-- title:
--   Theorem 2.9 — both weighted lower bounds (corrected: finite witness accounting)
-- statement:
--   For scheduling on one machine with release dates and positive weights, both of the following worst-case lower bounds hold. In each case the instance can have all weights equal to one and all processing times strictly positive, with the canonical α-schedules generated by an actual optimal SRPT schedule $P$.
--
--   1. For every $\varepsilon>0$ there is a finite nonempty input $I$ with attained true optimal nonpreemptive weighted cost $\mathrm{OPT}(I)>0$, for which the shared uniform parameter $a\in(0,1]$ has integrable α-schedule cost and
--
--   $$\mathbb E_{a\sim\mathrm{Unif}(0,1]}\!\left[\sum_j w_j C^a_j\right]>(2-\varepsilon)\mathrm{OPT}(I).$$
--
--   2. For every $\varepsilon>0$ there is a possibly different such input with an attained best parameter $a_*$ over all $a\in(0,1]$, and
--
--   $$\min_{0<a\le1}\sum_j w_j C^a_j=\sum_j w_j C^{a_*}_j>\left(\frac43-\varepsilon\right)\mathrm{OPT}(I).$$
--
--   Optimality is explicitly against every feasible nonpreemptive schedule of the same weighted instance. The second inequality consequently holds for every α on that one instance, not for separate inputs chosen after α. Unit-weight witnesses establish worst-case lower bounds for the full weighted class without assuming an algorithm for weighted preemptive optimization. These are companion limits on the algorithms, not supports for the published goal.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: expected approximation ratio at least 2, and Best-α ratio at least 4/3 → two independent clauses, each giving an ε-close finite witness for every ε>0, with positive attained true nonpreemptive OPT; Best-α attains the minimum over every 0<α≤1 on the same input; reason: worst-case ratios are suprema over instances, not lower bounds on every instance or against the preemptive relaxation
--   - encoding: unit-weight zero-size witness illustrations on p.156 → unit-weight positive-size instances using the immutable model and its actual fixed-label SRPT trace; reason: positive perturbations preserve both asymptotic constants; the Best-α witness also moves the second batch release to 1+2h, tending to 1, so its bound is uniform in α
--   - correction: p.156 writes I(1/2, ε, n) and claims both α-regime costs equal 1+2n → the two-batch family I(1/2,n), with only the stated asymptotic 4/3 bound retained, not the erroneous finite-cost identity; reason: the family was defined with two arguments; in the second regime the unit job finishes at 3/2, so the zero-size cost is 3/2+2n, not 1+2n; the positive witness has uniform cost at least 2n and an explicit feasible comparison of 3n/2+2+h(3n+4), preserving the limit
-- source:
--   Chekuri, Motwani, Natarajan, Stein, Approximation Techniques for Average Completion Time Scheduling, SIAM J. Comput. 31(1), 2001, DOI 10.1137/S0097539797327180, pp. 155–156, Theorem 2.9

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_AvgCompletionSched_BestAlpha_Supplement_Objectives
import Definitions.Def_AvgCompletionSched_BestAlpha_Supplement_Events

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace AvgCompletionSched.BestAlpha.Supplement

open MeasureTheory

/-- Theorem 2.9, pp.155–156: both worst-case lower bounds, witnessed by positive-size,
unit-weight instances of the full weighted class. Comparison is with the attained true
NONPREEMPTIVE optimum. Best-alpha attains its minimum; the bound is uniform over all
alpha on the same instance, rather than a pointwise family of different bad instances. -/
theorem theorem_2_9 :
    (∀ ε : ℝ, 0 < ε →
      ∃ (n : ℕ) (_ : 0 < n) (I : Instance n) (P : PreemptiveSchedule I)
        (w : Fin n → ℝ) (Nopt : NonpreemptiveSchedule I),
        (∀ j, w j = 1) ∧ IsSRPT P ∧ OptimalPreemptive P ∧
        OptimalNonpreemptive w Nopt ∧ 0 < weightedCost w Nopt ∧
        IntegrableOn (alphaCost P w) (Set.Ioc (0 : ℝ) 1) ∧
        (2 - ε) * weightedCost w Nopt <
          ∫ a in Set.Ioc (0 : ℝ) 1, alphaCost P w a) ∧
    (∀ ε : ℝ, 0 < ε →
      ∃ (n : ℕ) (_ : 0 < n) (I : Instance n) (P : PreemptiveSchedule I)
        (w : Fin n → ℝ) (Nopt : NonpreemptiveSchedule I) (aBest : ℝ),
        (∀ j, w j = 1) ∧ IsSRPT P ∧ OptimalPreemptive P ∧
        OptimalNonpreemptive w Nopt ∧ 0 < weightedCost w Nopt ∧
        IsBestAlpha P w aBest ∧
        (4 / 3 - ε) * weightedCost w Nopt < alphaCost P w aBest) := by sorry

end AvgCompletionSched.BestAlpha.Supplement
